/* A reference a program has dropped stops keeping its object alive.
 *
 * The shape matters and is not incidental: the method contains a `try`, and the
 * emitter makes every local of such a method volatile, because a local assigned
 * inside a try and read in its catch has to survive the longjmp and the C
 * standard calls a non-volatile one indeterminate. Volatile means the compiler
 * may not drop the store, so `big`'s word in the frame keeps pointing at the
 * array for the rest of main, and a collector that reads the frame word by word
 * keeps 8 MB alive that nothing can reach.
 *
 * The number printed is the collector's own account of what the last collection
 * kept, in whole megabytes: 8 while the frame is scanned conservatively, 0 once
 * the compiler names the frame's reference words and the word of a reference
 * that is dead is cleared. Java 21 answers the same question with a null
 * (System.gc() then Runtime.freeMemory()), and Teyru has liveBytes() for it.
 *
 * The expectation is the answer with the frame maps on, which is the default:
 * `true` and `0`. Both configurations are runnable from the same source and the
 * same binary layout, and the difference is the whole point of the case:
 *
 *	teyru build -O1 -o /tmp/t245 t245_dropped_reference.teyru && /tmp/t245
 *	# true
 *	# 0
 *	TEYRU_NO_FRAME_MAPS=1 teyru build -O1 -o /tmp/t245c t245_dropped_reference.teyru
 *	/tmp/t245c
 *	# true
 *	# 8
 *
 * The suite runs the first form (the maps are the default), so the .expected
 * file is the 0. The second form is what the entry in AGENTS.md is about, and
 * it is why the collector's own live set -- not a wall-clock number -- is what
 * is printed: an over-retentive collector and a correct one differ by exactly
 * the array, and the array is 8 MB.
 *
 * The case is Teyru's, not Java's: java.lang.System has no liveBytes, so the
 * JDK differential has nothing to run (tests/jdk-diff-allow.txt says so).
 */
class DroppedReference {
  static long fill(int[] a) {
    long s = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < a.length : i++) {
      a[i] = i
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < a.length : i++) {
      s += a[i]
    }
    return s
  }

  static void churn(int n) {
    Object[] keep = new Object[64]
    for (int i = 0 : i < n : i++) {
      keep[i % 64] = new Object()
    }
  }

  public static void main(String[] a) {
    int[] big = new int[2 * 1024 * 1024]
    long sum = fill(big)
    try {
      churn(1000)
    } catch (Throwable t) {
      System.out.println(t)
    }
    churn(300000)
    System.gc()
    System.out.println(sum > 0)
    System.out.println(System.liveBytes() / (1024 * 1024))
  }
}
